    "reading": {
        "body": "Florida's catalogued data center footprint totals just 3 MW of confirmed capacity across 13 facilities, a figure so low it reflects incomplete reporting rather than the state's actual infrastructure scale.\n\nOnly one facility, Flexential Tampa - North, carries a power figure, and that single site accounts for all 3 MW in the dataset. The remaining 12 facilities have no MW values attached. Any comparison of Florida to other states, or any claim about the state's aggregate capacity, would rest almost entirely on that one number.\n\nThe geographic spread is real and visible: facilities cluster in Miami-Dade, the Fort Lauderdale-Sunrise corridor, Tampa, and Jacksonville, with the University of Florida operating two adjacent research computing sites in Gainesville. That university presence matters for AI specifically because academic supercomputing installations, like HiPerGator, often run GPU clusters under research mandates rather than commercial colocation terms, making them a distinct category from the carrier-neutral or wholesale facilities in the same list. The data does not distinguish power sources, grid interconnection points, or available capacity, so nothing here speaks to whether Florida's grid can support expansion.",
